>> title: Roasted Brussels Sprouts
>> category: Vegetables & Sides
>> tags: brussels sprouts, roast, balsamic, sheet pan, side
>> counters: The Bowl Shop
>> aka: roasted brussels sprouts, balsamic brussels sprouts, crispy brussels sprouts, roast sprouts, charred brussels
>> pairs-with: polenta, basic-vinaigrette
>> servings: 4
>> time: 40 min
>> step.1: trim and halve, loose leaves kept
>> step.2: toss in oil, salt and pepper
>> step.3: roast 425°F (220°C) 22 min, cut side down
>> step.4: simmer the balsamic to a syrup 4 min
>> step.5: toss off the heat

Trim the dry stem end off @Brussels sprouts{1 1/2%lb}(700 g) and halve each one through the root, keeping every loose leaf that falls off. Do not tidy those away. They go on the pan with everything else and come out as the crisp brown flakes that are the reason anyone eats this.

Toss @&(~1)halved sprouts{} with @olive oil{2%Tbs}(30 mL), @kosher salt{1%tsp} and @black pepper{1/2%tsp}, then turn every half cut side up in the bowl so the oil pools on the flat face.

Spread @&(~1)oiled sprouts{} cut side down on a #sheet pan{} heated with the oven and ~roast{22%min} at 425°F (220°C). Twenty-two minutes untouched: the cut face goes to mahogany and the round back stays green and firm, which is the whole contrast. Stir them at the halfway mark, as most recipes tell you to, and you get thirty-two evenly beige sprouts.

Simmer @balsamic vinegar{1/4%cup}(60 mL) with @honey{1%tsp} in a small pan ~simmer{4%min} until it coats the back of a spoon and pulls in a thread off the edge.

Toss @&(~2)roasted sprouts{} with @&(~1)balsamic syrup{} and @flaky salt{1/2%tsp} off the heat. Balsamic is a quarter sugar. Put it on at the start and twenty-two minutes at 425°F turns it to soot on the pan; put it on at the end and it clings to the crisp faces and stays sweet-sour.
